WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.040
"Do Healthy Fast Food Options
Lead to Healthier Choices?"

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:15.200
In 2017, and to much fanfare,
menu labeling for calorie counts

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:18.320
began to be mandated
in national chain restaurants.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:20.640
I mean shouldn't consumers
have the knowledge needed

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:23.360
to make healthy eating
choices outside the home?

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:26.320
It just makes sense that caloric
information on menus will help

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:30.640
consumers limit food intake
to stay within daily energy needs,

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:36.000
but it didn't work. It turns out
calorie labels are not effective,

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:41.200
perhaps shaving on average an
insignificant 8 calories off of a meal.

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:44.880
You could have totally 
predicted that. Why?

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:49.200
Just as one might divine the value
of front-of-pack traffic light labeling

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:52.800
from the ferocity of the food
industry response against it,

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:56.080
one could probably gauge
the futility of calorie labeling

00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:00.000
by the ease at which some
regulations have been passed.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.840
McDonald's voluntarily started
publishing calorie info

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:06.880
nationally back in 2012
after a labeling mandate

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:07.840
in New York City was found

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:10.960
to have no overall effect
on consumer behavior.

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:14.320
So studies suggest such labeling
could boost "perceptions of

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:16.800
the restaurant's concern
for consumers' well-being"

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:21.520
while carefully not undermining
any Big Mac attacks.

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:23.760
At the same time,
McDonald's announced plans

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:26.720
for adding seasonal 
produce to their menu.

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:31.600
How cynical do you have to be not to
at least recognize that as a good thing?

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:36.240
Ironically, adding a healthy option
can actually drive people

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:42.400
to make even worse choices.
Ready to have your mind blown?

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:45.760
If you offer people a choice of side
dishes—something unhealthy

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:49.200
like French fries or something more
neutral like a baked potato—

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:54.080
only about 10% of individuals with high
self-control will splurge for the fries.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:55.520
Good for them.

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:59.680
French fries are so unhealthy though
that as a public health do-gooder

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:03.760
you add a third option, an even
healthier option than the baked potato—

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:07.520
a side salad to appeal
to their better natures.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:10.160
So then, instead of choosing
between an indulgent choice

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:13.200
and the more neutral baked potato,
they have their pick of

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:17.920
the indulgent choice, the neutral
choice, or an even healthier choice.

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:21.120
Even if everyone doesn't choose
the salad, more will go for

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:24.480
the middle-ground baked potato
over the fries, right?

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:28.320
So how much farther does
French fry fancying fall

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:36.080
by adding the salad option to the
mix? It shoots up, tripling to 33%.

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:39.040
Without the salad option,
only 1 in 10 chose the fries,

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:43.440
but that jumped to a third of people
just at the sight of salad.

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:45.680
The same thing happens when
you offer people the choice between

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:49.120
a bacon cheeseburger, a chicken
sandwich, or a veggie burger.

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:52.160
In a "No Healthy Option"
scenario where people were

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:55.920
offered the cheeseburger, a chicken
sandwich, or a fish sandwich,

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.240
17% chose the burger.

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:01.200
Swap out the fish sandwich
for a veggie burger

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:05.360
and bacon cheeseburger
preference doubled to 37%.

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:10.640
How can just seeing a healthy option push
people to make unhealthier choices?

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:14.000
The paper described in these series
of experiments was entitled

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:18.480
"Vicarious Goal Fulfillment: When the
Mere Presence of a Healthy Option

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:21.200
Leads to an Ironically
Indulgent Decision."

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:23.920
The thought is that seeing
the salad or veggie burger,

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:28.720
people make the mental note to choose
that at some nebulous next time,

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:33.570
thereby giving them the
excuse to indulge now.

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:38.160
See, there's a fascinating glitch of
human psychology called self-licensing.

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:42.640
This is when we unwittingly justify
doing something that draws us away

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:46.000
from our goals after we've just done
something that brings us towards them,

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:51.360
like justifying eating a donut because
you lost so much weight last week.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:56.160
We reward ourselves with an
indulgence that sets us back.

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:58.960
If you give smokers
" vitamin C supplements,"

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:01.440
they subsequently
smoke more cigarettes

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:04.560
than if you give smokers what
you explain are "placebo" pills,

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:08.240
even though both groups were
given identical sugar pills.

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:11.040
The group who thought they were
taking supplements smoked

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:15.440
nearly twice as much, perhaps
thinking at some subconscious level

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:18.400
that since they had just done
something good for their health,

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:20.800
they could afford to "live a little,"

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:27.591
which may have in effect indeed
occasioned them to live a little less.

00:04:27.591 --> 00:04:30.560
You can see how this could
translate into our lifestyle arenas.

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:34.000
Those given placebo pills they
believed to be dietary supplements

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:37.360
not only expressed less desire to
subsequently engage in exercise,

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:40.320
but followed through by
walking about a third less.

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:43.200
Compared to those who were
told the pills were placebos,

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:46.560
misled participants were also
more likely to choose a buffet

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:49.520
over what was described
as a "healthy, organic meal."

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:51.200
Would they eat more too?

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:52.560
A seminal study entitled

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:56.320
"The liberating effect of weight loss
supplements on dietary control"

00:04:56.320 --> 00:04:57.981
put it to the test.

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:00.720
Participants were randomized
to take a known placebo

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:04.640
or a purported weight loss supplement—
actually just the same placebo—

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:07.520
and later covertly observed at a buffet.

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:10.640
Not only did the supplement
subjects eat more food,

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:15.280
they chose less healthy items.
They also ate about 30% more candy

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:18.880
in a bogus "taste test" and
ordered more sugary drinks.

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:22.080
Hence, the investigators
concluded, "people who rely

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:24.240
on dietary supplements
for health protection

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:29.847
may pay a hidden price: the curse
of licensed self-indulgence."

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:35.600
So, circling back, what the vicarious
goal fulfillment studies added is that

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:39.440
not only does making progress towards
a goal rationalize decision making

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:43.280
that undermines us, but even just
considering making progress

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:45.678
can have a similar licensing effect.

00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:47.920
Note the study subjects were
not only moved to make

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:52.560
the unhealthier choice,
but the unhealthiest choice.

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:55.840
You'd think that even if people didn't
go for the salad or veggie option,

00:05:55.840 --> 00:05:58.560
the presence of a healthier alternative
might at least have encouraged them

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:03.000
to choose something in-between: not
as healthy, but not the unhealthiest,

00:06:03.022 --> 00:06:06.800
but instead it moved people
in the opposite direction.

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:10.400
Compared to the "No Healthy Option"
of chocolate-covered Oreos,

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:15.840
regular Oreos, or golden Oreos,
adding a "lower-calorie" Oreo option

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:19.440
doubled the likelihood that the study
participants would go straight

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:22.720
for the most indulgent
chocolate-covered option.

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:26.800
This is attributed to another illogical
quirk of human psychology

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:30.480
indelicately named the
"what the hell effect."

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:35.040
This is when one forbidden cookie
can lead dieters to eat the whole bag.

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:36.800
Once you've already strayed
from your goals,

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:39.680
well then, why not go all the way?

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:42.320
So once people decide they're
going to get that salad next time

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:45.840
and spoil themselves just this once,
they might as well go for

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:48.160
the most indulgent choice.

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:51.920
The halo of healthy foods
can even warp our perceptions.

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:54.560
Show weight-conscious people
a burger and nothing else,

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:57.840
then ask them to estimate the number
of calories, and the average answer

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:01.280
is 734 calories.
Now show folks

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:04.640
the exact same burger
accompanied by 3 celery sticks,

00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:08.720
and they guess the total
comes out to 619 calories.

00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:11.040
Did they think the celery
had negative calories?

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:13.520
No, most knew the
celery had calories, too,

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:16.720
but just the juxtaposition
of the burger with the celery

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:18.400
made the burger seem healthier.

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:20.560
The same thing happens
when you add an apple

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:22.400
to a bacon-and-cheese
waffle sandwich,

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:25.600
a side salad to beef chili, or some
carrots next to a cheese steak.

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:28.080
About a hundred calories
appear to disappear.

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:30.480
Health halo effects may explain
why people are more likely

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:32.720
to order a dessert and
more sugary drinks

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:37.520
with a "healthier" sub at Subway
versus a Big Mac at McDonald's,

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:41.840
even though the sub used in the study—
filled with ham, salami, and pepperoni—

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:46.240
had 50% more calories to begin with.

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:48.960
Even just a reference
to healthy foods can do it.

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:53.360
Show people a picture of a Big Mac and
people estimate it has 646 calories.

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:56.480
Okay, then just add the words

00:07:56.480 --> 00:07:59.600
"For your health, eat at least five
fruits and vegetables per day,"

00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:03.440
and all the sudden, the same burger
in the same ad was thought

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:06.240
to only have 503 calories.

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:09.520
Merely offering or even
promoting salads and fruit

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:12.960
can bring McDonald's accolades
and bolster consumer loyalty

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:17.028
without, ironically,
helping their health.

